WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.640
In 1977 a drought killed most of the finches on a small island, and the survivors

2
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.300
had bigger beaks. Did the finches just evolve?

3
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:17.313
The island is Daphne Major, in the Galápagos.

4
00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:23.533
Researchers had banded and measured nearly every medium ground finch living there.

5
00:00:24.593 --> 00:00:27.193
So they knew each bird's beak before the drought began.

6
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:31.427
Picture each bird as a dot on a line of beak depth.

7
00:00:32.827 --> 00:00:35.327
Shallow beaks sit on the left, deep beaks on the right.

8
00:00:36.587 --> 00:00:39.727
Before the drought, the whole population makes this grey cloud.

9
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.200
Then the drought comes.

10
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.580
About 85 percent of the medium ground finches died.

11
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.180
The survivors were not a random sample.

12
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.300
Larger birds, especially males with larger beaks, survived best.

13
00:00:56.383 --> 00:01:02.423
Why those birds? The food left on the island was mostly large, hard seeds.

14
00:01:03.743 --> 00:01:07.283
Something was sorting the birds, and the seeds that were left are the leading

15
00:01:07.283 --> 00:01:08.283
suspect.

16
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:12.757
The survivors' average now sits to the right of the old average.

17
00:01:13.977 --> 00:01:16.297
That gap measures selection through survival.

18
00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:22.317
If the survivors breed about equally, it is the selection differential, called S.

19
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.930
So is that evolution?

20
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.930
Not yet shown. Survival tells you who lived.

21
00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.430
It cannot tell you what their chicks will look like.

22
00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:35.910
That depends on inheritance.

23
00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:42.613
Narrow-sense heritability, h², -squared, is the share of a trait's variation due to

24
00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:44.273
genes whose effects add up.

25
00:01:45.293 --> 00:01:48.513
That is the part that makes offspring resemble their parents.

26
00:01:49.713 --> 00:01:53.613
It runs from 0 to 1, in one population and one environment.

27
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:56.937
The survivors pair up and raise chicks.

28
00:01:58.637 --> 00:02:00.777
Suppose beak depth were not inherited at all.

29
00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:04.317
Where on the line would the survivors' chicks land?

30
00:02:10.783 --> 00:02:14.823
Right back under the old average, if the environment stays the same.

31
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:19.863
However strong the selection, the chicks' average would not move at all.

32
00:02:20.883 --> 00:02:25.423
Turn the dial all the way to one, and the chicks' average lands on their parents'

33
00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:29.563
average. For most real traits the dial sits in between.

34
00:02:30.883 --> 00:02:33.443
The chicks' average moves only part of the way.

35
00:02:34.483 --> 00:02:42.723
That part is the response, called R. The breeder's equation says R = h² × S. It

36
00:02:42.723 --> 00:02:45.443
predicts how far the next generation's average moves.

37
00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:49.503
So one sentence about a drought hides three claims.

38
00:02:51.143 --> 00:02:52.143
Selection happened.

39
00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:54.263
The trait is inherited.

40
00:02:55.543 --> 00:02:56.883
And the offspring changed.

41
00:02:58.103 --> 00:02:59.743
Each claim needs its own evidence.

42
00:03:00.817 --> 00:03:03.337
A survival record lights the first lamp.

43
00:03:04.677 --> 00:03:06.457
Comparing relatives lights the second.

44
00:03:07.737 --> 00:03:10.417
Only measuring the next generation lights the third.

45
00:03:11.637 --> 00:03:15.277
To show that selection changed a trait's average, you need all three.
